# The probability of the flip is the variable p, never assigned, so the bound is asked for
# every initial p; the flip is reached only where 0 <= p <= 1, where p is a probability, so no
# run reaches an out-of-range probability:
#   wlp(S, [c = 1]) = p   for 0 <= p <= 1,   and 0 elsewhere,
# so the cwlp upper bound 0.75 fails only where p > 0.75; the guard keeps p <= 0.5 there.
# Expected: valid.  The twin of flip_state_above_one_cwlp_upper_unknown.sgcl: the shifted
# error arm of the reduced lower bound costs no bound where the probability is in range.
c := 0;
if 0 <= p and p <= 0.5 {
	if flip(p) {
		c := 1
	}
} else {
	c := 0
}
return c
?Ex[[c = 1]] <= 0.75 cwlp
